[{:seon.task/name "one-content-keyed-derivation"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed by worktree session agent-view, 2026-10-23, at the owner's question: one universal cache, with easy rules for when to recompute, shared by every worker. Root's points of 2026-10-23 are folded in. It is a new mechanism, so it goes through root's gate (AGENTS §3) before it is built. It must meet AGENTS §5: \"Results are reused by input digest\" and \"Content-keyed reuse is allowed for units measured as expensive; no other caches, and never one keyed by time.\"

What exists. There are about a dozen separate reuse mechanisms, sharing nothing but `seon.error/digest`:
- test and check results by reach digest, in ../test-results and as :seon.proof/digest facts (`seon.proofs/plan`, `seon.prove`);
- main's reach graph, kept by the fork server and inherited copy-on-write (`seon.agent/base-graph`);
- Jolt's compile and AOT caches;
- bin/build stamps;
- the source-import digest;
- the render proc's per-unit input equality and its `:turn-ctx`;
- `seon.render.proc/ordered`, reused by identity or for 1000 ms: a reuse keyed by time, which §5 forbids;
- Datahike's node cache;
- small `delay`s.

v1's answer (~/src/seon/src/seon/cache.clj) was one per-process cache at about 84 sites:
- keyed [kind content-key], nothing invalidated;
- evicted by size under 512 MB;
- compute-once per key;
- a hit cost 150-220 ns, and the misses it saved averaged 90-650 ms;
- JVM-only (Caffeine), and never shared across processes.

The design: one mechanism, one tier, one store.
1. The key is the reach digest of what f reads (seon.program/reach-digests: one Merkle digest per strongly connected component, over content ids from global-ids). It comes from the reach machinery we already compute; no second hasher.
2. A result is a fact on main keyed by that digest (`:seon.derived/key`). Every branch and agent sees it the moment any one computes it: tests reused across sessions and worktrees. A large value lives in the store by content (konserve: \"a large value lives once under its own key\"), the fact pointing at it. Every process reads it through the same LMDB pages.
3. One tier. Datahike's and konserve's in-process caches over the store are the per-process tier. A second one is added only when a measured miss says the store read is the cost (§5).
4. Concurrent requests for one key are joined by the prove proc's join on one digest, extended rather than duplicated: a request is a message, and a duplicate parks on the first's answer.
5. The space is bounded by a config key in bytes (:seon.config.derive/max-bytes). Eviction is by size, never by time.
6. Only pure work derives. A process test, or anything that reads the clock, the network or a model, is refused, by the reach walk's own classification (what it marks as reaching a process), never by a hand list.

It replaces and deletes, in order of measured cost; each first user is a proof that a second request at the same digest does no work, measured in calls and ticks:
- (a) the ../test-results directory: results become facts. This also removes self-contained's step A, which only moves that directory.
- (b) per-file analysis in the plan: pure-tests' (c), 8-15 s a run.
- (c) the base test plan per run: pure-tests' (b); bin/test's selection takes 7-12 s on an unchanged tree.
- (d) the fork server's reach-graph cache, if it fits: 640 ms and 816 MB for a full graph.

Then, measured first: an attempt's sections (built twice per later turn, by `seon.context/attempt-text`), the help page (2,132 tokens reassembled per turn of every agent), a turn's remade context (`seon.render.proc/turn-text`), and `seon.render.proc/ordered`'s time window (deleted). pure-tests holds two caching tasks for this one and sends their numbers.

Correctness: a mutation in any input f reads changes the key (sensitivity), and a change in an input f does not read leaves it (precision).

Order: measure each candidate's miss cost (REPL, n ≤ 100); build the key and the fact with user (a); extend the prove proc's join; add (b)-(d) one at a time, each with its proof. Sized for one worktree session."
  :seon.task/kind :design
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.derive-test/a-mutated-input-changes-the-key
                      seon.derive-test/an-unread-input-leaves-the-key
                      seon.derive-test/a-second-request-at-a-digest-does-no-work
                      seon.derive-test/concurrent-requests-for-one-key-run-once
                      seon.derive-test/work-reaching-a-process-is-refused
                      seon.derive-test/the-space-is-bounded-by-size}}]
